Specification ImportQif

Description Reading a QIF export of twenty years of Quicken history: its
Description sections, its account and category lists, its transactions, its
Description splits, its transfers and its investment actions.
Details
Details This is the migration. It runs once, against a file nobody will produce
Details again, and whatever it gets wrong is wrong for twenty years. So it
Details reports everything it did, it refuses rather than guesses wherever a
Details guess would be unverifiable, and it can be run twice without doubling
Details the book.
Details
Details QIF carries both halves of every transfer. That is the single largest
Details hazard here, and the rule that collapses them is the heart of this file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shape of the file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
DataType QifSection
Description What an exclamation-mark header introduces.
Details Everything after a header belongs to that section until the next one.
Details The sections marked "observed" are the ones the two files in testdata
Details actually contain. ClassList and PriceList are not among them: Quicken
Details replaced classes with tags, and neither file carries a price list.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value           | Notes                                              |
| AccountList     | !Account -- observed                               |
| CategoryList    | !Type:Cat -- observed                              |
| TagList         | !Type:Tag -- observed; replaced ClassList          |
| ClassList       | !Type:Class -- not in either file                  |
| SecurityList    | !Type:Security -- not in either file               |
| PriceList       | !Type:Prices -- not in either file                 |
| MemorizedList   | !Type:Memorized -- observed                        |
| InvoiceItemList | !Type:InvItem -- observed; invoice line items      |
| TemplateList    | !Type:Template -- observed; a report definition    |
| BankTxns        | !Type:Bank -- observed                             |
| CashTxns        | !Type:Cash -- observed                             |
| CreditCardTxns  | !Type:CCard -- observed                            |
| AssetTxns       | !Type:Oth A -- observed                            |
| LiabilityTxns   | !Type:Oth L -- observed                            |
| InvestmentTxns  | !Type:Invst -- observed                            |
| InvoiceTxns     | !Type:Invoice -- observed; receivables             |
| BillTxns        | !Type:Bill -- observed; payables                   |
| TaxTxns         | !Type:Tax -- observed; one record in each file     |
| AutoSwitchOn    | !Option:AutoSwitch -- observed                     |
| AutoSwitchOff   | !Clear:AutoSwitch -- observed                      |
| Unknown         | Anything else; reported and skipped                |

BusinessRule How a header line is read
Description Case and trailing blanks are ignored, because exports differ in both.
Details Both files in testdata write "!Type:Bank " and "!Type:Cash " with a
Details trailing space and the others without, so trimming is not optional.
Examples: SectionHeader
| Line                | Section         | Notes                            |
| !Type:Bank          | BankTxns        |                                  |
| !Type:Bank~         | BankTxns        | Trailing space, as in both files |
| !Type:Cash~         | CashTxns        | Likewise                         |
| !type:bank          | BankTxns        | Case ignored                     |
| !Account            | AccountList     |                                  |
| !Type:Cat           | CategoryList    |                                  |
| !Type:Tag           | TagList         |                                  |
| !Type:CCard         | CreditCardTxns  |                                  |
| !Type:Oth A         | AssetTxns       | The space is part of the name    |
| !Type:Oth L         | LiabilityTxns   |                                  |
| !Type:Invst         | InvestmentTxns  |                                  |
| !Type:Invoice       | InvoiceTxns     |                                  |
| !Type:Bill          | BillTxns        |                                  |
| !Type:Tax           | TaxTxns         |                                  |
| !Type:InvItem       | InvoiceItemList |                                  |
| !Type:Template      | TemplateList    |                                  |
| !Option:AutoSwitch  | AutoSwitchOn    |                                  |
| !Clear:AutoSwitch   | AutoSwitchOff   |                                  |
| !Type:Budget        | Unknown         | Not imported; reported           |

Attributes SectionHeader
| Attribute | Type       | Default |
| Line      | String     |         |
| Section   | QifSection | Unknown |
| Notes     | String     | none    |

$WIP
BusinessRule What an account block means depends on AutoSwitch
Description An !Account block is a definition while AutoSwitch is on, and a
Description change of the current account while it is off.
Details A Quicken export of a whole file turns AutoSwitch on, lists every
Details account, turns it off, and then alternates !Account with a transaction
Details section once per account. Reading an !Account block as a definition in
Description the second half would lose the account context and put every
Details transaction in the first account.
Examples: AccountBlockMeaning
| AutoSwitch | Meaning           | Notes                                    |
| on         | Definition        | One of the accounts of the file          |
| off        | SwitchTo          | The transactions that follow are its own |

Attributes AccountBlockMeaning
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| AutoSwitch | String |         |
| Meaning    | String |         |
| Notes      | String | none    |

Scenario A single-account file with no account block at all
Description The simplest export, and the one a bank or an old Quicken produces.
Description There is no account name anywhere in it, so the user names the
Description account to import into, as for a CSV file.
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2024
T-45.00
PHarris Teeter
LGroceries
^
D01/31/2024
T3000.00
PAcme Payroll
LSalary
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then qif records read are : QifRecord
| Account         | Date       | Amount   | Payee         | CategoryText |
| Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | -45.00   | Harris Teeter | Groceries    |
| Assets:Checking | 2024-01-31 | 3000.00  | Acme Payroll  | Salary       |

Attributes QifRecord
Description One record of a transaction section, before it becomes postings.
| Attribute    | Type        | Default    |
| Account      | AccountPath |            |
| Date         | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Amount       | Money       | 0.00       |
| Payee        | PayeeName   | none       |
| CategoryText | Text        | none       |
| Memo         | Text        | none       |
| CheckNo      | CheckNumber | none       |
| ClearedText  | Text        | none       |

$WIP
Scenario A whole-file export switches account as it goes
Description The account list defines three accounts; the sections that follow
Description each name the account their transactions belong to. The second
Description !Account block is a switch, not a redefinition.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
DFirst National checking
^
NVisa
TCCard
^
!Clear:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2024
T-45.00
PHarris Teeter
LGroceries
^
!Account
NVisa
TCCard
^
!Type:CCard
D01/20/2024
T-89.99
PAmazon
LShopping
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets           | Checking   | true        |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities      | CreditCard | true        |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       |
# The roots come with them: adding an account fills in every level above it as a
# placeholder, and a filled-in parent takes the type of the child that caused it
# so that a roll-up in a report adds up. See the chart rules in Accounts.
Then qif records read are : QifRecord
| Account          | Date       | Amount | Payee         | CategoryText |
| Assets:Checking  | 2024-01-15 | -45.00 | Harris Teeter | Groceries    |
| Liabilities:Visa | 2024-01-20 | -89.99 | Amazon        | Shopping     |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Account types and where accounts land in the tree
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule How a QIF account type becomes an account type and a path
Description QIF names a type and a bare name. The tree needs a root, and which
Description root follows from the type.
Details The roots are fixed: Assets, Liabilities, Income, Expenses, Equity. A
Details user who wants other names renames them afterwards, which renames the
Details descendants too.
Details Every type below occurs in the two files in testdata. Invoice, Bill and
Details Tax are Quicken Home and Business accounts: receivables, payables, and a
Details tax holding account. The first two have types of their own now that the
Details Business group exists, which says what they are rather than only which
Details side of the sheet they fall on. Tax is an asset and nothing more needs
Details modelling about it for the balances to come out right.
Details
Details The first column is the word in the file, which is Quicken's and not
Details ours. QIF writes Bank where this program now says Checking.
Examples: QifAccountMapping
| QifType | AccountType        | Root        | ExampleName | ExamplePath          |
| Bank    | Checking           | Assets      | Checking    | Assets:Checking      |
| Cash    | Cash               | Assets      | Wallet      | Assets:Wallet        |
| CCard   | CreditCard         | Liabilities | Visa        | Liabilities:Visa     |
| Oth A   | OtherAsset         | Assets      | House       | Assets:House         |
| Oth L   | OtherDebt          | Liabilities | Mortgage    | Liabilities:Mortgage |
| Invst   | Brokerage          | Assets      | Brokerage   | Assets:Brokerage     |
| Port    | Brokerage          | Assets      | Portfolio   | Assets:Portfolio     |
| Invoice | AccountsReceivable | Assets      | Receivables | Assets:Receivables   |
| Bill    | AccountsPayable    | Liabilities | Payables    | Liabilities:Payables |
| Tax     | OtherAsset         | Assets      | Tax         | Assets:Tax           |

Scenario A credit limit on a card account is kept
Description Forty-three of the card accounts in the business file carry one. It
Description is not a balance and posts nothing; it is kept so that a register
Description can show how much of the card is used.
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NVisa
TCCard
L5000.00
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then account limits are : AccountLimit
| Account          | CreditLimit |
| Liabilities:Visa | 5000.00     |

Attributes AccountLimit
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| Account     | AccountPath |         |
| CreditLimit | Money       | 0.00    |

Attributes QifAccountMapping
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| QifType     | String      |         |
| AccountType | AccountType | Checking |
| Root        | AccountName |         |
| ExampleName | AccountName |         |
| ExamplePath | AccountPath |         |

$WIP
Scenario An account name containing a colon is kept whole
Description QIF account names are free text and a colon in one would otherwise
Description split it into two levels of the tree. The colon is replaced on the
Description way in, and the change is reported.
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NSavings: Rainy Day
TBank
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                        |
| Assets:Savings - Rainy Day  |
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind         | Detail                                                      |
| NameChanged  | Account "Savings: Rainy Day" became "Savings - Rainy Day"   |

Attributes MigrationNote
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Kind      | Text |         |
| Detail    | Text |         |

$WIP
Scenario An account with a statement balance records an opening balance
Description The QIF account block may carry the balance and the date it was
Description taken. It is not an opening balance and is not used as one: it is
Description kept as the figure to check the import against, as a downloaded
Description ledger balance is in ImportOfx.
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
/12/31/2024
$1705.00
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then statement balance to check is : QifStatementBalance
| Account         | StatedBal | StatedBalAt |
| Assets:Checking | 1705.00   | 2024-12-31  |

Attributes QifStatementBalance
| Attribute   | Type        | Default    |
| Account     | AccountPath |            |
| StatedBal   | Money       | 0.00       |
| StatedBalAt | Date        | 2024-12-31 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The category list
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
Scenario The category list says which categories are income and which expense
Description This list is read before any transaction, because it is the only
Description thing in the file that says whether Bonus is income or an expense.
Description Without it the sign of the first transaction has to decide, which
Description is a guess that will be wrong somewhere in twenty years.
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Cat
NSalary
DGross pay
I
T
^
NAuto
DVehicle
E
^
NAuto:Fuel
E
^
NBonus
I
T
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Income:Bonus       | Income  | false       |
| Income:Salary      | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
# Expenses:Auto is not a placeholder: the file declares it as a category in
# its own right, and transactions may be posted to it. A category that only
# appears as a parent is made a placeholder, as in Accounts.

$WIP
BusinessRule How a category name becomes an account path
Description Using the income or expense flag from the category list. A name not
Description in the list falls back to the sign of the amount, and that fallback
Description is reported every time it is used.
Examples: CategoryMapping
| CategoryText | InList  | Amount  | AccountPath          | Fallback | Notes                     |
| Groceries    | Expense | -45.00  | Expenses:Groceries   | false    |                           |
| Salary       | Income  | 3000.00 | Income:Salary        | false    |                           |
| Auto:Fuel    | Expense | -52.00  | Expenses:Auto:Fuel   | false    | Subcategory keeps its depth |
| Bonus        | Income  | -100.00 | Income:Bonus         | false    | The list wins over the sign |
| Mystery      | Absent  | -45.00  | Expenses:Mystery     | true     | Money out, so an expense  |
| Mystery      | Absent  | 45.00   | Income:Mystery       | true     | Money in, so income       |
|              | Absent  | -45.00  | Expenses:Uncategorized | false  | No category at all        |
|              | Absent  | 45.00   | Income:Uncategorized   | false  |                           |

Attributes CategoryMapping
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| CategoryText | Text        | none    |
| InList       | String      |         |
| Amount       | Money       | 0.00    |
| AccountPath  | AccountPath |         |
| Fallback     | Boolean     | false   |
| Notes        | String      | none    |

$WIP
Scenario Every use of the sign fallback is reported
Description A category that was not declared is the sort of thing worth looking
Description at once, so each distinct name is listed with a count rather than
Description one line per transaction.
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Cat
NGroceries
E
^
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2024
T-45.00
LGroceries
^
D01/16/2024
T-12.00
LMystery
^
D01/17/2024
T-13.00
LMystery
^
"""
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When qif file imported
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind              | Detail                                                       |
| CategoryGuessed   | "Mystery" was not in the category list; made Expenses:Mystery from the sign, 2 transactions |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dates
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule How a QIF date is read
Description QIF dates are the worst part of the format. The order of the day and
Description the month is stated in a profile, as for a CSV file, and never
Description guessed. The apostrophe is Quicken's own century marker.
Details Every date in both files in testdata uses the apostrophe form, and
Details every part of it may be blank-padded rather than zero-padded -- the
Details year included, which is the form that is easiest to get wrong. The
Details year tokens observed run from " 5" to "26" without a gap, so an
Details apostrophe year is two thousand plus the number, for one digit and for
Details two alike.
Details The slash form with a two-digit year does not occur in either file. It
Details is kept, reading by the window rule of ImportCsv, because an older
Details export would use it.
Examples: QifDateParse
| DateOrder | Text        | Date       | Valid | Notes                            |
| MDY       | 3/12'17     | 2017-03-12 | true  | The commonest shape              |
| MDY       | 1/ 2'18     | 2018-01-02 | true  | Blank-padded day                 |
| MDY       | 12/ 9'25    | 2025-12-09 | true  | Two-digit month, padded day      |
| MDY       | 6/17' 5     | 2005-06-17 | true  | Blank-padded year: 2005          |
| MDY       | 1/ 2' 9     | 2009-01-02 | true  | Both padded                      |
| MDY       | 1/31'26     | 2026-01-31 | true  | The latest in the business file  |
| MDY       | 3/ 7'24     | 2024-03-07 | true  | The date of the one stock split  |
| MDY       | 1/15'05     | 2005-01-15 | true  | Zero-padded year, same reading   |
| MDY       | 01/15/2024   | 2024-01-15 | true | Four-digit year, slash form      |
| MDY       | 1/15/24     | 2024-01-15 | true  | Slash form, window rule          |
| MDY       | 12/31/99    | 1999-12-31 | true  | Window rule: 99 is 1999          |
| DMY       | 15/01/2024   | 2024-01-15 | true | The other order                  |
| DMY       | 01/15/2024   | 2024-01-01 | false | No fifteenth month              |
| MDY       | 02/29/2024   | 2024-02-29 | true | A leap year                      |
| MDY       | 02/29/2023   | 2024-01-01 | false | Not a leap year                 |
| MDY       |             | 2024-01-01 | false | A record needs a date            |

Scenario A file that proves its own date order is not queried
Description The business file has 24,324 dates whose second part is above
Description twelve and none whose first part is, so it can only be month first.
Description Where the file settles the question the profile is not consulted and
Description the user is not asked.
Given the qif profile is : QifProfile Vertical
| DateOrder | MDY |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D3/12'17
T-45.00
PShop A
^
D1/31'26
T-20.00
PShop B
^
"""
Then date order is ambiguous : DateOrderWarning Vertical
| Ambiguous | false |
Then date order evidence is : DateOrderEvidence Vertical
| FirstPartOver12  | 0   |
| SecondPartOver12 | 1   |
| Proven           | MDY |

Attributes DateOrderEvidence
| Attribute        | Type      | Default |
| FirstPartOver12  | Integer   | 0       |
| SecondPartOver12 | Integer   | 0       |
| Proven           | DateOrder | MDY     |

$WIP
Scenario A file that contradicts the profile it was given is refused
Description If the file proves DMY and the profile says MDY, one of them is
Description wrong and importing either way would be a guess.
Given the qif profile is : QifProfile Vertical
| DateOrder | MDY |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D25/12'17
T-45.00
PShop A
^
"""
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                             |
| The file proves DMY order and the profile says MDY; nothing imported |

Attributes QifDateParse
| Attribute | Type      | Default    |
| DateOrder | DateOrder | MDY        |
| Text      | String    | none       |
| Date      | Date      | 2024-01-01 |
| Valid     | Boolean   | true       |
| Notes     | String    | none       |

Scenario A file whose dates could be read either way stops before importing
Description The same rule as for a CSV file, and it matters more here: a whole
Description history read in the wrong order is not something that gets noticed
Description by looking at one register.
Given the qif profile is : QifProfile Vertical
| DateOrder | MDY |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D03/04/2024
T-10.00
^
D05/06/2024
T-20.00
^
"""
Then date order is ambiguous : DateOrderWarning Vertical
| Ambiguous      | true       |
| UsingOrder     | MDY        |
| FirstDateAsMDY | 2024-03-04 |
| FirstDateAsDMY | 2024-04-03 |

Entity QifProfile
Description The few things about a QIF file that cannot be read from it.
| Attribute      | Type      | Default  | Notes                              |
| DateOrder      | DateOrder | MDY      | Never guessed                      |
| DecimalComma   | Boolean   | false    | For a European export              |
| InvestmentMode | InvestmentMode | CashOnly | What to do with trade history |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Amounts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule How a QIF amount is read
Description Newer exports write the amount twice, as U and as T. T is used, and
Description a disagreement between the two is reported rather than resolved.
Examples: QifAmountParse
| Text        | DecimalComma | Amount  | Valid | Notes                        |
| -45.00      | false        | -45.00  | true  |                              |
| 3000.00     | false        | 3000.00 | true  |                              |
| 1,234.56    | false        | 1234.56 | true  | Thousands separator          |
| -1,234.56   | false        | -1234.56 | true |                              |
| 45          | false        | 45.00   | true  | No decimal point             |
| -45,00      | true         | -45.00  | true  | A European export            |
| 1.234,56    | true         | 1234.56 | true  | Separators the other way     |
|             | false        | 0.00    | true  | An empty amount is zero      |
| abc         | false        | 0.00    | false | Not a number                 |

Attributes QifAmountParse
| Attribute    | Type    | Default |
| Text         | String  | none    |
| DecimalComma | Boolean | false   |
| Amount       | Money   | 0.00    |
| Valid        | Boolean | true    |
| Notes        | String  | none    |

Scenario A record whose U and T amounts disagree is reported
Description They are the same field written twice and they should never differ.
Description When they do, something has rewritten the file, and that is worth
Description knowing before twenty years of it is trusted.
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2024
U-45.00
T-45.50
PHarris Teeter
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind           | Detail                                                  |
| AmountMismatch | 2024-01-15 Harris Teeter: U is -45.00 and T is -45.50; used -45.50 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cleared status
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule How the C field becomes a cleared status
Description Quicken has written four different markers over the years and they
Description mean two things between them.
Examples: ClearedMapping
| Text | Cleared    | Notes                       |
|      | Uncleared  | Absent or empty             |
| *    | Cleared    |                             |
| c    | Cleared    | Lower case, newer files     |
| X    | Reconciled |                             |
| R    | Reconciled | Lower case R also seen      |

Attributes ClearedMapping
| Attribute | Type          | Default   |
| Text      | String        | none      |
| Cleared   | ClearedStatus | Uncleared |
| Notes     | String        | none      |

Scenario A cleared marker is applied to the posting in its own account only
Description The marker belongs to the account whose section the record is in,
Description which is exactly the posting-level status of Transactions. The
Description other side of the transaction is left uncleared.
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2024
T-45.00
PHarris Teeter
LGroceries
CX
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount | Cleared    |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 | Reconciled |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  | Uncleared  |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule The split lines decide the categories, not the L field
Description A split record carries S, E and dollar lines for its real categories,
Description and its L field holds the literal text "--Split--". Taken as a
Description category name that text would create an account called --Split--
Description with a thousand transactions in it.
Details All 1,026 records in the business file whose L is "--Split--" have
Details dollar lines, and 19 records have dollar lines while L holds a real
Details category. So the rule is not "ignore L when it says --Split--" but
Details "ignore L whenever there are split lines", which covers both.
Examples: SplitCategorySource
| LField             | HasSplitLines | CategoriesFrom | Notes                        |
| --Split--          | true          | SplitLines     | 1,026 records in the business file |
| Groceries          | true          | SplitLines     | 19 such records; L is ignored |
| Groceries          | false         | LField         | An ordinary transaction      |
| --Split--          | false         | None           | Never seen; reported if met  |
|                    | true          | SplitLines     |                              |

Attributes SplitCategorySource
| Attribute      | Type    | Default |
| LField         | Text    | none    |
| HasSplitLines  | Boolean | false   |
| CategoriesFrom | String  |         |
| Notes          | String  | none    |

Scenario A split record keeps --Split-- out of the chart of accounts
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D4/ 2'24
T-154.50
PHome Depot
L--Split--
SHome:Maintenance
$-100.00
SHome:Garden
$-54.50
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts
| Path | Type | Placeholder |
# No rows: every account named was already there, and --Split-- is not a name.
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee      | Account                   | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Assets:Checking           | -154.50 |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00  |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50   |

BusinessRule How a split line amount becomes a posting amount
Description The dollar lines are signed from the point of view of the account the
Description record is in, and they sum to the T amount. A posting to the category
Description is therefore the dollar value with its sign turned round, and the
Description posting to the account itself is T.
Details This is confirmed rather than assumed: in all 1,045 records with dollar
Details lines across both files, the lines sum exactly to T. In 268 of them the
Details lines do not all share one sign -- a paycheque has gross pay one way
Details and deductions the other -- so the rule is per line, not per record.
Examples: SplitLineSign
| T        | Dollar1  | Dollar2  | Dollar3 | AccountPosting | Posting1 | Posting2 | Posting3 | Notes          |
| -154.50  | -100.00  | -54.50   |         | -154.50        | 100.00   | 54.50    |          | A payment      |
| 3550.00  | 5000.00  | -1200.00 | -250.00 | 3550.00        | -5000.00 | 1200.00  | 250.00   | A paycheque    |
| -45.00   | -45.00   |          |         | -45.00         | 45.00    |          |          | One line       |

Attributes SplitLineSign
| Attribute      | Type  | Default |
| T              | Money | 0.00    |
| Dollar1        | Money | 0.00    |
| Dollar2        | Money | 0.00    |
| Dollar3        | Money | 0.00    |
| AccountPosting | Money | 0.00    |
| Posting1       | Money | 0.00    |
| Posting2       | Money | 0.00    |
| Posting3       | Money | 0.00    |
| Notes          | String | none   |

Scenario A split record becomes one transaction with a posting per line
Description The S, E and dollar fields repeat, one group per split line, and
Description they come after the fields of the transaction itself.
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D04/02/2024
T-184.50
PHome Depot
SHome:Maintenance
EPaint
$-100.00
SHome:Garden
ECompost
$-54.50
SAuto:Repairs
EWiper blades
$-30.00
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee      | Account                   | Amount  | Memo         |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Assets:Checking           | -184.50 | none         |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00  | Paint        |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50   | Compost      |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00   | Wiper blades |
# The split amounts carry the sign of the account side in QIF, and are turned
# round to become category postings, as a register entry is in Transactions.

Scenario A split whose lines do not add up to the total is reported and balanced
Description Refusing the record would lose it. The difference goes to
Description Uncategorized, where it is visible and can be put right, and the
Description record is listed in the migration report.
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized    | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D04/02/2024
T-184.50
PHome Depot
SHome:Maintenance
$-154.50
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee      | Account                   | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Assets:Checking           | -184.50 |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 154.50  |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Uncategorized    | 30.00   |
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind         | Detail                                                        |
| SplitShort   | 2024-04-02 Home Depot: split lines are 30.00 short of 184.50; put to Uncategorized |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transfers, and the duplicate halves
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm TransferHalf : QifRecord
Description A record whose category field is an account name in square
Description brackets. Quicken writes one in each of the two accounts, so a
Description transfer appears in the file twice.

$WIP
BusinessRule When two transfer halves are the same transfer
Description The accounts must cross-match and the amounts must be equal and
Description opposite. The dates need not agree, and where several halves could
Description pair the nearest date wins.
Details This rule was wrong until the real export was measured against. It
Details required the dates to be the same, and of the 4,025 pairs in the
Details business file only 3,053 share one: 558 are a day apart, 174 two, 180
Details three, 41 four or five, and 19 more than ten days apart.
Details That mattered far more than a count suggests. A half with no partner
Details still posts both of its sides, because the bracketed name tells us both
Details accounts -- so each of the 972 pairs the rule missed had its transfer
Details counted twice, once from each half, in both accounts. The card balances
Details came out tens of thousands of dollars wrong.
Details The nearest date is what keeps two separate transfers of one amount
Details between one pair of accounts pairing with the right halves.
Examples: TransferPairing
| Test | Condition                                                          |
| 1    | One names the account of the other in brackets, and the other names the first |
| 2    | The amounts are equal and opposite                                 |
| 3    | Neither has already been paired with something else                |
| 4    | Of those that remain, the one whose date is nearest                |

Attributes TransferPairing
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Test      | Integer | 0       |
| Condition | String  |         |

Scenario Both halves of a transfer become one transaction
Description This is the rule that stops the migration doubling every transfer
Description in twenty years of history.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
NSavings
TBank
^
!Clear:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D03/01/2024
T-500.00
PTransfer to savings
L[Savings]
^
!Account
NSavings
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D03/01/2024
T500.00
PTransfer from checking
L[Checking]
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee               | Account         | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer to savings | Assets:Checking | -500.00 |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer to savings | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New       | 1 |
| Duplicate | 1 |
| Matched   | 0 |
| Possible  | 0 |
# One transaction created, one half recognised as its other side. The payee of
# the half that was kept is the one from the account the money left.

Scenario Two transfers of the same amount on the same day stay two transfers
Description Both are real. Each half pairs with exactly one other half, and
Description once a half is paired it is not available to pair again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
NSavings
TBank
^
!Clear:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D03/01/2024
T-500.00
PTransfer one
L[Savings]
^
D03/01/2024
T-500.00
PTransfer two
L[Savings]
^
!Account
NSavings
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D03/01/2024
T500.00
PIn one
L[Checking]
^
D03/01/2024
T500.00
PIn two
L[Checking]
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Account         | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer one | Assets:Checking | -500.00 |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer one | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  |
| T2  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer two | Assets:Checking | -500.00 |
| T2  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer two | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New       | 2 |
| Duplicate | 2 |
| Matched   | 0 |
| Possible  | 0 |

$WIP
Scenario A transfer half with no partner still becomes a whole transfer
Description The bracketed account names both sides, so the transaction can be
Description made from one half alone. It happens when only some accounts were
Description exported, and it is reported because the other account may be
Description about to be imported from a second file.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given the file contains
"""
!Option:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
!Clear:AutoSwitch
!Account
NChecking
TBank
^
!Type:Bank
D03/01/2024
T-500.00
PTransfer to savings
L[Savings]
^
"""
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
| Assets:Savings  |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee               | Account         | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer to savings | Assets:Checking | -500.00 |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Transfer to savings | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  |
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind            | Detail                                                      |
| UnpairedTransfer | 2024-03-01 Assets:Checking to Assets:Savings 500.00: only one half was in the file |

Scenario Dates a day apart do pair
Description Quicken does not always write both halves with the same date: 558 of
Description the pairs in the business file are exactly one day apart. Leaving
Description them unpaired counted each of those transfers twice.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
