| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Span      | Text    |         |
| FromYear  | Integer | 2006    |
| ToYear    | Integer | 2026    |
| Invoices  | Integer | 0       |
| Note      | Text    | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Things the two files in testdata settled
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description The rules in this section were written from the files rather than
Description from the format, and each says what was observed.

$WIP
BusinessRule What encoding a QIF file is read as
Description Tried as UTF-8 first; read as Windows-1252 if that fails.
Details The two files differ. pughkilleen.QIF is valid UTF-8, with the
Details registered-trade-mark sign written as two bytes. kenpugh.QIF is not
Details valid UTF-8: it contains a lone byte 0x96, which is an en dash in
Details Windows-1252 and an illegal lead byte in UTF-8. Reading either as the
Details wrong one of the two either fails or produces mojibake, so the choice
Details is made by trying, not by asking.
Examples: EncodingChoice
| Observed                       | ReadAs       | Notes                        |
| Decodes as UTF-8               | UTF8         | pughkilleen.QIF              |
| Fails to decode as UTF-8       | Windows1252  | kenpugh.QIF, byte 0x96       |
| Begins with a UTF-8 byte order mark | UTF8    | Mark removed; neither file has one |

Attributes EncodingChoice
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Observed  | String |         |
| ReadAs    | String |         |
| Notes     | String | none    |

$WIP
BusinessRule A field whose value will not parse is a continuation of the one before
Description QIF has no way to quote a value, so a value containing a newline
Description becomes a line that begins with whatever character follows it. A
Description line beginning with D whose value is not a date is not a date.
Details This happens only in the invoice sections of these files: thirteen such
Details lines in kenpugh.QIF, inside !Type:Invoice. Elsewhere every D parses.
Details The D fields that look wrong in !Type:Cat and !Account are not
Details continuations at all: D means Description in those sections, which is
Details the other reason a field code cannot be read without knowing the
Details section it is in.
Examples: ContinuationLine
| Section       | Line                     | Reading                        |
| !Type:Invoice | D4/ 2'24                 | The date of the record         |
| !Type:Invoice | Development work, phase 2 | Appended to the field before it |
| !Type:Invoice | Dec 14-16, 2024          | Appended; not a date           |
| !Type:Cat     | DVehicle expenses        | The description of a category  |
| !Account      | DFirst National checking | The description of an account  |

Attributes ContinuationLine
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Section   | String |         |
| Line      | String |         |
| Reading   | String |         |

$WIP
BusinessRule What a field code means depends on its section
Description The same letter is a different field in a different section, which is
Description why the reader tracks the section and never reads a record alone.
Examples: FieldCodeBySection
| Code | InTransaction | InCategoryList | InAccountList   |
| D    | Date          | Description    | Description     |
| T    | Amount        | TaxRelated     | AccountType     |
| N    | CheckNumber   | Name           | Name            |
| L    | Category      | Unused         | CreditLimit     |
| E    | SplitMemo     | IsExpense      | Unused          |
| I    | Price         | IsIncome       | Unused          |
| R    | Unused        | TaxSchedule    | TaxDeferred     |

Attributes FieldCodeBySection
| Attribute      | Type   | Default |
| Code           | String |         |
| InTransaction  | String |         |
| InCategoryList | String |         |
| InAccountList  | String |         |

